From: Swift Linux <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] gallivm: deduce the StringMap iterator type

lp_build_fill_mattrs names llvm::StringMapIterator<bool> explicitly to walk
the feature map that llvm::sys::getHostCPUFeatures returns. LLVM 22 removed
that name: StringMap's iterators are now StringMapIterBase<ValueTy, IsConst>,
reached through StringMap<ValueTy>::iterator, and "StringMapIterator" does
not appear anywhere in ADT/StringMap.h. So the build stops at

    ../src/gallium/auxiliary/gallivm/lp_bld_misc.cpp:334:15: error:
      'StringMapIterator' is not a member of 'llvm'; did you mean
      'StringMapKeyIterator'?
      334 |    for (llvm::StringMapIterator<bool> f = features.begin();

and takes mesa3d with it, which every gallium driver built with LLVM
depends on.

The compiler already knows the type, so let it deduce one rather than
spell out a name that has changed twice. This keeps working whatever the
iterator is called next, and needs no LLVM_VERSION_MAJOR guard - the
surrounding code already carries one for the LLVM 19 change to
getHostCPUFeatures' signature, and adding a second for a purely spelled
type would be noise.

Nothing else about the loop changes, because nothing else had to:
operator* still yields StringMapEntry<ValueTy>&, StringMapEntry::first()
still returns the key as a StringRef, and second is still the value - all
three verified against LLVM 22.1.8's ADT/StringMap.h and ADT/StringMapEntry.h.
Only the name of the iterator broke.

This block is inside #if DETECT_ARCH_ARM, which detect_arch.h defines from
__arm__ alone, so it is 32-bit ARM that fails to build; aarch64 takes the
DETECT_ARCH_AARCH64 path and x86 the DETECT_ARCH_X86 one, neither of which
names the iterator type.

--- a/src/gallium/auxiliary/gallivm/lp_bld_misc.cpp
+++ b/src/gallium/auxiliary/gallivm/lp_bld_misc.cpp
@@ -331,7 +331,7 @@
       llvm::sys::getHostCPUFeatures(features);
    #endif

-   for (llvm::StringMapIterator<bool> f = features.begin();
+   for (auto f = features.begin();
         f != features.end();
         ++f) {
       MAttrs.push_back(((*f).second ? "+" : "-") + (*f).first().str());
